# AWOW bash backup service — configuration (WI-10.10). Copy to
# /etc/homehub-backup/backup.env (or next to backup.sh) and fill in. NOTHING here is
# committed with real values — this .example carries placeholders only. Prefer a
# root-only cifs credentials file over inline passwords.

# ── external-drive target (step 4) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The USB drive attached to the AWOW. Dated run snapshots (run_<UTC>) live here.
BACKUP_TARGET=/mnt/backup-drive
# Staging dir for the pulled sources before archiving (fast local disk).
BACKUP_STAGING=/var/tmp/homehub-backup/staging
# Retention: keep the last N run snapshots, prune older (rotation).
BACKUP_KEEP=7

# ── drive power / spin-down (WI-10.10 DRIVE POWER DESIGN) ─────────────────────
# The backup drive(s) are the box's biggest electrical lever (5–8 W each while
# spinning ≈ the whole CPU). Policy = DYNAMIC standby: backup-standby.service
# sets a conservative default spin-down timeout at boot, and the backup RUN
# disables standby on its target drive(s) at start + RESTORES the timeout on exit
# (trap; fires on failure/interrupt too). This avoids BOTH wear modes — no
# start/stop churn mid-run, and no aggressive-timeout cycling.
#
# Drive(s) to manage — SPACE-SEPARATED /dev/disk/by-id/... paths. Use by-id,
# NEVER sdX: the kernel renumbers sdX across boots/hotplug, by-id is stable.
# EMPTY = the whole feature is a clean no-op (no boot standby, no run-time hold).
#   ls -l /dev/disk/by-id/    # find the stable path for your USB backup enclosure
BACKUP_DRIVE_DEVICES=""
# e.g. BACKUP_DRIVE_DEVICES="/dev/disk/by-id/usb-Seagate_Expansion_0AC1234-0:0 /dev/disk/by-id/usb-WD_Elements_25E1-0:0"
#
# At-rest spin-down timeout — the hdparm -S value. The encoding is notoriously
# confusing, so spelled out:
#     0        = standby DISABLED (never spins down) — what the run uses mid-backup
#     1..240   = value × 5 seconds        (so 240 = 20 min)
#     241..251 = (value − 240) × 30 min   (so 241 = 30 min, 242 = 60 min, …)
# Recommended conservative default: 241 (= 30 min). 240 (= 20 min) is also fine.
BACKUP_DRIVE_STANDBY=241
#
# CAVEAT: many USB-SATA enclosures do NOT honor hdparm APM/standby (the bridge
# chip swallows or fakes the command). When that happens this is a HARMLESS no-op
# — power management NEVER fails a backup (missing hdparm, absent device, or a
# rejecting enclosure → logged WARNING, the run continues). Verify per drive at
# hardware BURN-IN:  hdparm -C /dev/disk/by-id/...   (shows active/idle vs standby;
# spin a drive down with `hdparm -y` and confirm it reports 'standby').

# ── INGEST (step 1b) — mirror network shares INTO the library tree ────────────
# RATIFIED 2026-07-29 and the INTENDED pattern for anything that lives on
# another box: the LIBRARY holds the current copy of a network source, and the
# ordinary archive flow below then covers that library folder like any other
# directory. One `name=//host/share -> /abs/library/dest` per line:
#   name    names the ingest in the run log (NOT a backup set name)
#   //host/share  the network source (cifs); it is mounted read-only
#   /abs/dest     where the copy LIVES in the library — an ABSOLUTE path
# Lines starting with # are skipped. The wake pre-step below runs FIRST, so a
# source box that sleeps is awake before the first mount.
#
# MIRROR SEMANTICS — READ THIS ONCE: the sync is `rsync -a --delete`, so the
# library copy is made to MATCH the share. A file DELETED on the share is
# DELETED from the library on the next run; the library is not an accumulating
# archive. History lives in the dated run snapshots under BACKUP_TARGET
# (BACKUP_KEEP of them), not in the library.
INGEST_SOURCES="gamebox=//mini-serv/mini-pc-share -> /srv/library/NonDocs/MiniServ"
# EMPTY = no ingest at all (sources are then pulled straight into staging, the
# legacy pattern). Which library paths are correct is answered by the pathing
# SSOT (Personal\deploy\storage-map.md), not by guessing from this example.
#
# MIRROR SAFETY: if a share MOUNTS but turns out to hold no files (wrong share
# name, a host that came up with its data drive unmounted), mirroring it would
# ERASE a good library copy — so the run REFUSES and fails loudly instead. Set
# this to true only for a share that is legitimately empty sometimes.
INGEST_ALLOW_EMPTY=false

# ── sources (step 1c) — ONE table, one `name=SPEC` per line (SR-013) ───────────
# Names become the archive/set names; every set flows through the same
# archive→hash→retention→report pipeline. SPEC forms:
#   path:/abs/dir           a local directory on this box — INCLUDING the library
#                           folders an INGEST_SOURCES line just refreshed (the
#                           intended pattern: ingest once, back up like any dir)
#   //host/share            cifs pull straight over the LAN into staging — the
#                           ORIGINAL form, still fully supported, but it leaves
#                           no current copy in the library; prefer ingest +
#                           `path:` above
#   volume:VOL              docker named volume on THIS box, copied live
#   volume:VOL@CONTAINER    stop CONTAINER for the copy, restart immediately
#                           after — for volumes holding live databases. The EXIT
#                           trap restarts it even if the run dies mid-copy.
# Lines starting with # are skipped. volume: specs need the docker CLI (a given
# on the AWOW). Consistency: a live volume copy can catch a DB mid-write —
# prefer @CONTAINER for actual/technitium (downtime = the copy, seconds).
# The Windows game box exposes ONE share — see the wake knobs below, it sleeps.
#
# The table also carries the per-set EXCLUDE lines (see BACKUP_EXCLUDE below):
#   name.exclude=PATTERN [PATTERN ...]
# A `name.exclude=` line naming a set that does not exist FAILS the run — a
# filter that silently excludes nothing is worse than no filter.
BACKUP_SOURCES="gamebox=path:/srv/library/NonDocs/MiniServ
gamebox.exclude=NameOfSomeVeryLargeFolder
# LEGACY alternative to the ingest+path pair above — pull the share directly:
# gamebox=//mini-serv/mini-pc-share
# The box's OWN service state (OI-8 / SN-010) — uncomment to protect it:
# actual=volume:actual_data@actual
# technitium=volume:technitium_config@technitium
# caddy=volume:caddy_data
# tracker=volume:tracker_data@tracker
# finance=volume:finance_snapshots@finance-auditor
"
# PRIVACY (Finance-Auditor §3 firewall): the `finance` set is RAW FINANCIAL
# DATA — it may ride the LOCAL backup drive but must NEVER be added to
# OFFSITE_SETS below, and its library path must never be one of the folders the
# IceDrive client is pointed at (that folder goes to the CLOUD; raw finance data
# never leaves the LAN).

# ── wake the source box (step 1 pre-step) — IT IS ALLOWED TO SLEEP ───────────
# The Windows game box sleeps to save power, so the nightly run WAKES it over
# the LAN and waits for its SMB port before mounting anything. The contract:
#   * the magic packet is best-effort, but the WAIT is the truth — the run only
#     proceeds once the box actually answers tcp/445;
#   * a wake TIMEOUT FAILS THE RUN LOUDLY (ok=false to NagLight, nonzero exit).
#     A source that never woke must NEVER be mistaken for a source with nothing
#     new to copy — that is the whole point of never-silent-green.
#
# MAC address of the source box's LAN adapter — EMPTY = the feature is entirely
# off (a box that never sleeps needs none of this). Find it on the Windows box
# with `getmac /v` (use the wired adapter; Wi-Fi rarely wakes reliably).
# ALSO REQUIRED ON THE WINDOWS SIDE: enable "Wake on Magic Packet" + "Allow this
# device to wake the computer" on the adapter, and turn OFF Fast Startup —
# hybrid shutdown leaves the NIC unable to wake.
BACKUP_WAKE_MAC=""
# e.g. BACKUP_WAKE_MAC="00:00:5E:00:53:01"        (placeholder — not a real MAC)
#
# Host or IP to PROBE for wakefulness (tcp/445 — the SMB port the ingest/pull
# needs). Use the same host name that appears in the //host/share above (in
# INGEST_SOURCES, or in a legacy BACKUP_SOURCES entry), or its reserved IP.
BACKUP_WAKE_HOST=mini-serv
# How long to wait for that port after sending the packet, in seconds. Generous
# is fine — it costs nothing when the box is already awake (the probe short-
# circuits) and a cold resume + service start can take a while.
BACKUP_WAKE_TIMEOUT=120
#
# OPTIONAL: broadcast address for the magic packet (default 255.255.255.255).
# Set a subnet-directed broadcast if your segment needs it. NOTE: bash cannot
# set SO_BROADCAST on its /dev/udp socket, so some kernels refuse a broadcast
# destination — if the log says no method worked, `apt-get install wakeonlan`
# (or etherwake) and the fallback takes over automatically.
# BACKUP_WAKE_BROADCAST=255.255.255.255
# BACKUP_WAKE_IFACE=            # only used by the etherwake fallback

# cifs credentials for the LAN Samba shares (one Samba local user per D5) — used
# by both the INGEST mounts and any legacy //host/share source. Use a credentials
# FILE (root-only, `username=`/`password=` lines) rather than inline.
BACKUP_CIFS_CREDENTIALS=/etc/homehub-backup/cifs.creds
# ...or inline (less safe):
# BACKUP_CIFS_USER=awow
# BACKUP_CIFS_PASS=REPLACE
BACKUP_CIFS_EXTRA=vers=3.0

# ── compression (step 2) — auto-compression-where-applicable ─────────────────
# A set whose bytes are >= THRESHOLD% already-compressed is stored as plain .tar;
# otherwise zstd (.tar.zst). Mirrors the FileBackup already-compressed exemption.
BACKUP_ZSTD_LEVEL=10
BACKUP_INCOMPRESSIBLE_THRESHOLD=60
# BACKUP_INCOMPRESSIBLE_EXT="jar zip 7z gz zst png jpg mp4 sav ..."   # override the default set

# ── exclusions (step 2) — what NOT to archive ─────────────────────────────────
# GLOBAL patterns, space-separated globs, applied to EVERY set. Passed to rsync
# (so the copy is never made) and to tar (so the archive can never contain them).
# Pattern semantics are rsync's: a pattern with no `/` matches that name at ANY
# depth, so `*.bak` catches editor backups everywhere and `SomeFolder` catches a
# directory of that name wherever it sits. Quote the whole list; do NOT let the
# shell see the globs unquoted.
BACKUP_EXCLUDE="*.bak"
# Other sensible candidates — uncomment/extend rather than guessing later:
# BACKUP_EXCLUDE="*.bak *.tmp *.part ~$* Thumbs.db desktop.ini .DS_Store node_modules __pycache__"
#
# PER-SET patterns live as extra lines in the BACKUP_SOURCES table above:
#   gamebox.exclude=NameOfSomeVeryLargeFolder AnotherHugeDir
# They ADD to the global list for that set (they do not replace it). This is
# where a named very-large folder belongs — one set's problem, one line.
#
# NOTHING IS EXCLUDED SILENTLY. Every run logs the effective pattern list per
# set, logs each path the patterns actually hid (the first few inline, all of
# them in `<set>.excluded.log` next to the archive), records the patterns in the
# MANIFEST's `excludes` column, and `restore.sh` says out loud that such a set is
# a FILTERED copy of its source. An excluded path is therefore never a mystery —
# if something is missing from a restore, the run dir says why.
# EMPTY / unset BACKUP_EXCLUDE + no per-set lines = nothing excluded anywhere.

# ── offsite (step 5) — RETIRED from the target state (Owner, 2026-07-29) ──────
# THE CORRECTED MODEL: the backup service does NO offsite staging at all. The
# IceDrive client (running in the SR-015 opt-in desktop session) is pointed
# DIRECTLY at the chosen library paths in its OWN GUI and syncs them to the
# cloud itself. Nothing here copies, stages, or duplicates anything for it —
# which also means there is no second copy of the data on this box, and no
# per-run offsite folder to prune.
#
# So the target state is simply:
OFFSITE_ENABLED=false
#
# Consequences worth knowing:
#   * WHICH folders go to the cloud is configured in the IceDrive client, not
#     here — the authoritative list lives in Personal\deploy\storage-map.md §4e.
#     Never point it at a library path holding raw finance data.
#   * The client is a GUI app: sync is DOWN after every reboot until one desktop
#     session is opened (stack/remote-ui/README.md). Unlike a backup step, that
#     failure does NOT show up in this service's NagLight report — the feed can
#     be green while the cloud copy is stale.
#
# ── the legacy knobs are GONE, and setting one now REFUSES the run ────────────
# `OFFSITE_PATH`, `OFFSITE_UNC` and `OFFSITE_SETS` were carried as "legacy, still
# functional" from the 2026-07-29 design change until 2026-08-09, when the Owner
# ruled to delete the step. The code is gone.
#
# The knobs are still RECOGNISED, on purpose. Setting any of them fails the run
# at start with a message naming the knob — because the alternative is a box
# whose config asks for an offsite copy and gets a backup that quietly has none,
# and a config silently ignored is the same family of fault as a green run that
# wrote nothing. `OFFSITE_ENABLED=false` above stays legal and inert.

# ── target preflight (step 0) — the drive must really be there ────────────────
# true (default) = refuse to run unless BACKUP_TARGET is a REAL MOUNTPOINT.
#
# Why this defaults to true: the generated fstab mounts the backup drive with
# `nofail` (mandatory — a missing USB disk must not hold up local-fs.target and
# drop a headless box to an emergency shell). The cost is that an unplugged
# drive leaves BACKUP_TARGET as a perfectly good EMPTY DIRECTORY on the system
# disk. Without this check the run creates its dated folder there, copies into
# it, verifies it, prunes old runs, and reports ok=true — a green backup lane
# writing the household's data to the wrong disk until that disk fills.
#
# The check reads /proc/self/mountinfo only: no disk I/O, so it cannot wake a
# spun-down drive. A read-only mount (NTFS dirty bit) also refuses.
#
# Set to false ONLY if BACKUP_TARGET is deliberately a plain directory on an
# always-present filesystem. The run then warns loudly every time.
BACKUP_TARGET_REQUIRE_MOUNT=true

# ── the LIBRARY backup (library-backup.sh + the FileBackup container) ─────────
# A SECOND SERVICE SHARING THIS FILE, not a second copy of the one above. It
# takes the nine library `path:` sets — the 4 TB tree with no archive today —
# and gives them per-file dedup plus Snapshot_<date> history. The bash service
# above keeps ingest, the volume: sets, the Mini-serv set, WoL and drive power;
# both run, on separate timers. The HOST PATHS the container binds are NOT here:
# they live in stack/.env as FILEBACKUP_SOURCE/STATE/BACKUP/CHANGES/LOGS,
# because that is the file docker compose interpolates when it mounts them.
#
# THE FLOOR IS THE WHOLE SPACE POLICY (Q-FB2, the Owner 2026-08-23: retention
# NONE). Snapshots are kept indefinitely and library-backup.sh prunes NOTHING —
# so a breach of this floor is a hard REFUSAL posted RED, not a warning, and it
# will not clear itself. The relief valve is manual, and the refusal message
# spells it out (`snapshots`, then `prune -Snapshot <name>`, dry-run first).
# Every successful run also carries the drive's fill percentage in its feed
# summary, so the trend is readable long before the floor is in sight.
#
# Whole GB of FREE space required on the backup drive before a run may start.
# 100 is chosen for an 8 TB archive drive holding a ~4 TB library: enough that a
# large day's changes cannot fill the disk mid-run, small enough not to refuse a
# drive that is merely well used. Lower it if the archive drive is smaller.
BACKUP_LIBRARY_MIN_FREE_GB=100
# And on the filesystem holding /state (the manifest cache) and /logs — usually
# the SYSTEM DISK, where filling it stops Docker and every service on the box,
# not just the backup. Both are small; the floor only has to be non-trivial.
BACKUP_LIBRARY_MIN_FREE_STATE_GB=2
# The weekly `verify` gate, as an ISO weekday (1 = Monday … 7 = Sunday). The
# FIRST occurrence of this weekday each month runs `verify -Deep` (which also
# proves payload identity and so reads the data files); the other occurrences
# run a plain manifest verify; every other day skips. Read from the calendar
# rather than a state file — a state file on the backup drive is lost with the
# drive, and one on the system disk drifts from it.
BACKUP_LIBRARY_VERIFY_DAY=7
# The dedicated NagLight lane for this service. NOT `backup` (above): one of
# these two going red must never be able to look like the other going green.
LIBRARY_BACKUP_FEED_CHECK=library-backup

# ── reporting (step 6) — NagLight /api/feed, never-silent-green ───────────────
# The backup-ran feeder successor. On success posts ok=true; on ANY failure the
# service posts ok=false and exits nonzero.
# The tracker is bridge-only (D2: no host publish), so the deployed form runs
# the POST inside the tracker container (busybox wget via docker exec) against
# its own loopback; the public https route would bounce through oauth2-proxy
# and overwrite X-Forwarded-User. Unset NAGLIGHT_FEED_CONTAINER only where the
# URL is genuinely host-reachable (sim / single-user).
NAGLIGHT_FEED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/feed
NAGLIGHT_FEED_CONTAINER=tracker
NAGLIGHT_FEED_CHECK=backup
# Bearer token for /api/* POSTs (TRACKER_API_TOKEN on the tracker).
NAGLIGHT_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_TRACKER_API_TOKEN
# Multi-user tracker (D3): the stable Google `sub` to attribute the item to,
# sent as X-Forwarded-User directly to the tracker (the documented trust model —
# the feeder is a backend, not a browser). Leave blank for a single-user tracker.
NAGLIGHT_USER=
